🗓️ 7-Day Beginner DSA Plan (from scratch)
Goal: learn the habit → Brute force → dry run → find repeated work → optimize → remember pattern

Daily rule (every problem):
1) Restate problem in 1 line
2) Write brute force
3) Dry-run on a tiny example
4) Say time complexity (O(n), O(n²), …)
5) Ask: “What work is repeated?”
6) Write smarter version only if needed
7) Save both methods in one Java file (like ProductOfArrayExceptSelf)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Day 1 — Warm-up + complexity intuition
Focus: loops, arrays, O(n) vs O(n²)

Do / revise in your folder:
- Factorial.java
- Fibonacci.java
- PrimeEvenOdd.java
- ArmstrongNumber.java
- ElementWiseArraySum.java

Learn today:
- O(1), O(n), O(n²) meaning with examples
- Nested loop over same array ≈ usually O(n²)
- Read: Time_Complexity_BigO

Practice tasks:
1) Sum of array
2) Find max/min
3) Reverse array in-place

Interview question to answer aloud:
“Why is nested loop often O(n²)?”

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Day 2 — Strings + simple scanning
Focus: char arrays, two-end thinking

Do / revise:
- ReverseString.java
- Palindrome.java
- Whitespace_Uppercase_LowerCase.java

Practice tasks:
1) Count vowels
2) Check anagram (brute: sort both; smart later with count array/HashMap)
3) First non-repeating character (brute first)

Pattern seed:
- Two pointers from start/end (palindrome/reverse)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Day 3 — HashSet / HashMap basics (very important)
Focus: uniqueness + frequency counting

Do / revise:
- FindCommonElementBetweenTwoArrays.java
- IBMissingNumberProblem.java

Practice tasks:
1) Contains duplicate?
2) Two Sum (brute O(n²), then HashMap O(n))
3) Majority / frequency of elements

Pattern:
- “Need fast lookup / count” → HashMap/HashSet

When to choose HashMap smart way:
If brute checks every pair / rescans array for each element.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Day 4 — Prefix idea (your ProductExceptSelf day)
Focus: precompute to avoid recomputation

Do / revise deeply:
- ProductOfArrayExceptSelf.java  ← master this
- CountStep.java

Practice tasks:
1) Prefix sum array: prefix[i] = arr[0]+...+arr[i]
2) Range sum query using prefix (brute vs O(1) after preprocess)
3) Running product from left

Pattern:
- Answer at i needs “all left” and/or “all right” → prefix/suffix

Decision rule:
Brute recomputes left/right many times → use prefix/suffix.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Day 5 — Two pointers + sliding window intro
Focus: sorted arrays / subarrays

Practice tasks:
1) Pair with given sum in sorted array (two pointers)
2) Remove duplicates from sorted array
3) Max sum of subarray of size k (brute, then sliding window)

Pattern:
- Sorted + pair/search from ends → two pointers
- Contiguous window of size k → sliding window

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Day 6 — Searching + sorting mindset
Focus: why sorting helps

Practice tasks:
1) Linear search vs binary search (on sorted array)
2) Merge two sorted arrays
3) Intersection of two arrays (brute, then sort+two pointers or HashSet)

Learn:
- Binary search only on sorted data
- Sorting costs O(n log n), then many problems become easier

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Day 7 — Recursion basics + revision
Focus: think in smaller subproblems

Do / revise:
- Fibonacci.java (recursive + iterative)
- Factorial.java

Practice tasks:
1) Print 1..n with recursion
2) Sum of digits recursively
3) Climb stairs (1 or 2 steps) — brute recursion first

Then revision day:
- Re-solve ProductOfArrayExceptSelf without looking
- Re-solve Two Sum brute + HashMap
- Explain out loud: when brute is enough vs when optimize

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 How to decide “smart way” (cheat sheet)

| You notice this...                         | Try this pattern      |
| ------------------------------------------ | --------------------- |
| Nested loops recalculating same values     | Prefix / suffix       |
| Searching if value exists many times       | HashSet               |
| Counting frequency / pairs                 | HashMap               |
| Sorted array + pair from ends              | Two pointers          |
| Contiguous subarray of fixed/variable size | Sliding window        |
| Need sorted order repeatedly               | Sort first            |
| Problem size n ≤ 1000 and no constraint    | Brute is OK to start  |
| n = 10^5 or asked O(n)                     | Must optimize         |

Beginner golden rule:
If you cannot explain brute force dry-run, do NOT jump to smart solution.

